   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.3",
    "topic_title": "Estimating Limit Values from Graphs",
    "stem": "A function f is described by its graph on 0 ≤ x ≤ 4. The graph is a line segment from (0, 1) rising to (2, 3) and a line segment from (2, 3) falling to (4, 1). Both segments end in an open circle at (2, 3). There is also a single filled dot at (2, 5). Which statement about f is true?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "lim(x→2) f(x) = 3 and f(2) = 5",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Tracing the graph toward x = 2 from the left, the y-values approach 3. Tracing from the right, they also approach 3. Because both one-sided limits equal 3, the two-sided limit is 3. The filled dot only records the function value, f(2) = 5, which does not affect the limit."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "lim(x→2) f(x) = 5 and f(2) = 5",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This uses the filled dot as the limit value. A limit describes the y-values the graph approaches near x = 2, not the y-value plotted at x = 2. Both sides of the graph head toward 3, not 5. Fix: Cover the point at x = 2 and trace each side of the graph toward it before reading any dot."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "lim(x→2) f(x) does not exist and f(2) = 5",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This assumes that a limit fails to exist when the plotted point does not match where the graph is heading. A two-sided limit exists whenever the left-hand and right-hand limits are equal, and here both equal 3. Fix: Find the left-hand and right-hand limits separately, then ask only whether those two values match."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "lim(x→2) f(x) = 3 and f(2) = 3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This treats the open circle at (2, 3) as the function value. An open circle marks a y-value that the graph approaches but does not reach. The filled dot at (2, 5) is the actual value, so f(2) = 5. Fix: Check which point at x = 2 is filled, and use only that point when reading f(2)."
     }
    ]
   },
