   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.3",
    "topic_title": "Estimating Limit Values from Graphs",
    "stem": "The graph of f near x = 2 is described as follows: For 1 ≤ x < 2, a straight line segment runs from (1, 1) toward an open circle at (2, 3). For 2 < x ≤ 3, another straight line segment runs from an open circle at (2, 5) to (3, 4). There is a filled point at (2, 7), and there are no other graph pieces near x = 2. What is lim(x → 2) f(x)?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Left-side-only trap: The left branch approaches 3, but the right branch approaches 5. A two-sided limit requires both sides to approach the same value. Fix: Trace toward x = 2 from the right as well as the left, and compare the approached y-values."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "5",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Right-side-only trap: The right branch approaches 5, but the left branch approaches 3. Reading only the right branch does not establish a two-sided limit. Fix: Trace toward x = 2 from the left as well as the right, and compare the approached y-values."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The limit does not exist.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Tracing the graph toward x = 2 from the left gives y-values approaching 3; tracing from the right gives y-values approaching 5. Since the one-sided limits differ, the two-sided limit does not exist, regardless of the filled point at (2, 7)."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "7",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Function-value trap: The filled point shows f(2) = 7, not what nearby graph values approach. Here the two branches approach different heights, so the filled point cannot determine the limit. Fix: Ignore the filled point initially and trace the nearby graph toward x = 2 from both sides."
     }
    ]
   },
